<p>
  A digital tachometer looks like a simple instrument, and that is why the cheapest ones tempt buyers. The mistakes show up later: a range that stops short of your fastest shaft, a display that drifts, or a tool that cannot do the one job you bought it for. Use this checklist when you compare digital tachometers in India, whether you buy for a workshop, a plant maintenance team, a college lab or resale.
</p>

<h2 id="range">1. Range Against Your Fastest and Slowest Shaft</h2>

<p>
  List the slowest and fastest speeds you will measure, including start-up and any variable-speed drive extremes. Contact tachometers usually stop lower than photo ones: the Systems Tech HTM 590 covers 60 to 50,000 RPM by contact, while the HTM 560 reaches 1,00,000 RPM without touching. Leave headroom above your top speed.
</p>

<h2 id="method">2. Contact, Non-Contact or Both</h2>

<p>
  Decide how you will reach the shaft. Our <a href={u('/blog/contact-vs-non-contact-tachometer/')}>contact vs non-contact guide</a> walks through it. If your jobs vary, a combined model such as the HTM 890 avoids buying two instruments.
</p>

<h2 id="surface">3. Do You Need Surface Speed?</h2>

<p>
  Conveyors, rollers, winders, paper and textile lines are specified in metres per minute. Only a contact tachometer with a wheel tip reads that directly. The HTM 590 and HTM 890 read 6 to 5000 m/min.
</p>

<h2 id="accuracy">4. Accuracy and Time Base</h2>

<p>
  Look for a quartz crystal time base and a stated accuracy such as 0.05% + 1 digit. Check that the resolution suits your speeds: 0.1 RPM below 1000 RPM is helpful on slow machinery.
</p>

<h2 id="features">5. Memory, Display and Power</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Last, maximum and minimum recall lets you read the value after the machine is clear.</li>
  <li>A 5-digit bright LED display stays readable in a workshop.</li>
  <li>Standard AA cells are easy to replace anywhere. Auto shut-off protects the batteries.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="kit">6. What Comes in the Box</h2>

<p>
  Check for contact tips (cone, cup, wheel), reflective tape, a carrying case and a manual. A tachometer without the right tip or tape cannot do the job.
</p>

<h2 id="source">7. Buy From an Authorised Distributor</h2>

<p>
  Buy from a supplier that can confirm the model, provide a proper tax invoice and give you the manufacturer's datasheet. CIE Instruments (Cambridge Instruments &amp; Engg. Co., Howrah and Kolkata) is an authorised distributor of Systems Tech tachometers and sensors for India.
</p>

